text:
alex: Quick sanity check on the rerank API contract before I finalize the PR. Two small items: 1) how do we want to treat missing/null `score` in responses (compat with old clients), and 2) tie-breaking policy — deterministic enough for tests?

maya: For scores: prefer always returning a numeric `score` when possible. If model can't score an item, return `null` rather than omitting the field. That keeps the shape stable.

alex: OK — callers prefer numeric, but legacy clients choke on `null` sometimes. Can we also document that `null` implies "unscored" and will be considered less than any finite score?

li: Yes — contract we agreed on in api-docs: treat `null` as -INF for ordering. But we should not mutate client-visible responses; just define ordering server-side. So response still contains `null`. Also add a note: `NaN` is not allowed; we reject requests with `NaN` in any numeric fields.

sam: Tie-breaking: we currently sort by score desc, then by `payload_order` (input index) as stable tiebreak. If `payload_order` missing, we fall back to `rank_seed` (request-level string) hashed with item id to deterministic int. That keeps results reproducible across replicas. Is that what you want?

alex: Yup. One more: for clients that rely on stable id order across reruns, should we expose the final tie key in the response? e.g. `tie_key` or `rank_hint`?

maya: We can add an optional `rank_hint` string field in response for debug/qa only. It will be omitted unless `?debug=true` or request header `X-RERANK-DEBUG: 1`. Not enabled by default.

alex: Perfect. Can you confirm sample request/response shape so I can wire up tests?

maya: Sample — minimal request and two possible responses:

```json
// Request
{
  "items": [
    {"id": "a", "text": "foo"},
    {"id": "b", "text": "bar"}
  ],
  "rank_seed": "canary-2026-03",
  "params": {"max_results": 5}
}

// Response (normal)
{
  "results": [
    {"id": "b", "score": 0.87, "rank": 1},
    {"id": "a", "score": 0.87, "rank": 2} // same score, stable by payload_order
  ]
}

// Response (item unscored)
{
  "results": [
    {"id": "b", "score": null, "rank": 2},
    {"id": "a", "score": 0.12, "rank": 1}
  ]
}
```

li: Couple implementation notes: 1) sorting uses (score==null ? -INF : score) desc, then payload_order asc, then hash(rank_seed + id) asc. 2) rank field is 1-based position in returned list. 3) telemetry emits `tie_break_reason` tag: "score", "payload_order", or "seed_hash".

sam: Also add a small compatibility paragraph in the API changelog: "Old clients that expect omitted `score` should still work; we will keep backwards-compatibility by accepting both omitted and null but will standardize on `null` in new responses."

alex: Thanks — I'll update the PR to: always include `score` (nullable), define ordering semantics (null -> -INF), stable tiebreak by payload_order then seed-hash, optional `rank_hint` under debug, and telemetry tags.

dev-bot: deploy-canary: rerank-schema-v2 canary started for 10% traffic.

maya: Nice — can we also add a unit test that fakes two items with identical scores and missing payload_order to exercise seed-hash fallback?

alex: Added to my branch, will push shortly and ping reviewers. Thanks!
